<p>President Trump intensified criticism of German Chancellor Friedrich Merz after Merz said the United States was being “humiliated” by Iran and lacked a clear strategy in the two-month-old war. Trump wrote on Truth Social that Merz should spend more time trying to end the Russia-Ukraine war and address Germany’s immigration and energy problems, and less time criticizing efforts to remove what Trump called the Iranian nuclear threat. The dispute widened after Trump said Washington was studying a possible reduction of U.S. troops in Germany, where American forces have been stationed for decades. He later suggested the same pressure could extend to other European troop-hosting allies over their positions on the Iran war. German officials emphasized continued military cooperation with the United States and said any redeployment decision should be handled with allies rather than through a public dispute.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ttQ_jUpZROD5uM75V9TmswCrqWRh1xUs7rBmzwp8PANQEFfrxR-nw-LL47nzOblIWN0ZvMSuMT6X0iujDYOoZuMFwhO-AmQn7qx2-0_H8484NNfVkvto' alt='President Trump met with German Chancellor Friedrich Merz in the Oval Office in March.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Pentagon reaction: Politico reported that Trump’s troop-cut remarks surprised Pentagon officials because a recent global force posture review had not recommended major European pullbacks.</li><li>Germany spending: Pentagon officials had recently praised Germany as a NATO ally increasing defense spending, with Berlin expected to raise outlays to 3.7% of GDP by 2030.</li><li>Iran pressure: The Trump administration has criticized several NATO members for not joining U.S.-Israeli military efforts against Iran, including by sending naval forces to help reopen the Strait of Hormuz.</li></ul><blockquote>We are prepared for that, we are discussing it closely and in a spirit of trust in all NATO bodies, and we are expecting decisions from the Americans. - Johann Wadephul</blockquote><h3>Perspectiv